      "side": 1,
      "message": "[From Jetski review] Because this uses `ArgsLike::unpack_value_opt` and returns `Ok(None)` when an element fails to unpack, passing a list with an invalid element (like `[123]` in `providers.rs`) now reports:\n`Expected list[Args | str], but got list (repr: [123])`\ninstead of the more specific element error that `FrozenArgsSequence` used to produce:\n`Expected Args | str, but got int (repr: 123)`\n\nIf we want to keep the element-level error message while still reporting `Expected list[Args | str]` for non-lists, we can use `type Error \u003d starlark::Error;` and `ArgsLike::unpack_value_err` after checking `ListRef`:\n```rust\nimpl\u003c\u0027v\u003e UnpackValue\u003c\u0027v\u003e for ArgsList\u003c\u0027v\u003e {\n    type Error \u003d starlark::Error;\n\n    fn unpack_value_impl(value: Value\u003c\u0027v\u003e) -\u003e Result\u003cOption\u003cSelf\u003e, Self::Error\u003e {\n        let Some(list) \u003d \u003c\u0026ListRef\u003e::unpack_value_opt(value) else {\n            return Ok(None);\n        };\n        Ok(Some(Self(\n            list.iter()\n                .map(ArgsLike::unpack_value_err)\n                .collect::\u003cResult\u003cVec\u003c_\u003e, _\u003e\u003e()?,\n        )))\n    }\n}\n```\nConversely, if returning `Ok(None)` (`Infallible`) is intentional, this implementation is equivalent to `Ok(UnpackList::\u003cArgsLike\u003c\u0027v\u003e\u003e::unpack_value_opt(value).map(|l| Self(l.items)))`.",
